// @check
// @error return type mismatch: expected boolean, got string | boolean
// FALSE-REJECT xfail (ROADMAP A5 / A6). This program is SOUND and SHOULD compile
// and run; it is rejected. The `xfail-false-reject-` prefix is the corpus's
// rejects-too-much kind — see README.vl. NOT a soundness hole: nothing unsound is
// accepted, a valid program is refused.
//
// THE GAP: a *sequence* of two divergent guard-clauses over a 3-way value union
// narrows the fall-through to the last remaining variant — here `x` is `boolean`
// after subtracting `i32` then `string`. `postGuardNarrowings` applies only ONE
// union subtraction per place per block (`typecheck.vl`, the GUARD CLAUSE
// comment), and the front end rejects the second. (The single-subtraction case —
// one guard over a 2-way union — works: see is-value-kind-union-sound.vl,
// narrowing-null-guard.vl.)
//
// ── THE REASON ON THE GATE IS REFUTED. Re-measured 2026-08-23. ──────────────────
//
// The gate used to say: "Neither backend can materialize the twice-subtracted residual
// of a tagged value union." That is FALSE of the native emitter, which is now the only
// one. Deleting the `pgKeys` veto and rebuilding, all of these COMPILE AND RUN CORRECTLY:
//
//   • this file's own program — `f(true)` is `true`, and all four members answer right;
//   • THREE subtractions over `string | i32 | boolean | f64`, residue `f64` → `4.5`;
//   • a STRUCT arm as the residue (`string | i32 | P`, then `x.px`) → `42`;
//   • a null STRIP followed by two `is` subtractions;
//   • the residual USED, not just returned (`return y + 1` on a twice-subtracted `i32`,
//     an unboxed `i32.add` that only validates if the narrowing reached the emitter).
//
// The emit side needed no change at all: `pushPostGuardNarrow`'s complement pushes STACK
// (see its header), so a second subtraction is already just a second push. The claim was
// written against the TS host, which is deleted, and nothing re-ran it.
//
// ── SO WHY IS THIS STILL OPEN? A SECOND RULE, NEVER NAMED ON THIS FILE. ─────────
//
// Subtracting CONSISTENTLY changes what an exhaustive `match` after a guard chain means.
// A `match` arm naming a member the guards already subtracted is a TYPE ERROR — that rule
// is not new, and this compiler's own source documents it in as many words:
//
//     `TyErr` has NO ARM because it cannot: the early `s is TyErr { return true }`
//     NARROWS `s`, so `TyErr` is not a member of the scrutinee's type here and an
//     arm for it is a type error.          — typecheck.vl, `assignable`
//
// `assignable` is guarded by SIX such clauses and then matches on `s`, spelling the other
// five as empty "claimed above" arms — which is only legal because the veto silently
// dropped their subtractions. With the veto gone the compiler STOPS COMPILING ITSELF:
//
//     compiler/typecheck.vl:14075:4: match pattern `TyVar` is not a member of
//       TyPrim | TyObj | TyFunc | TyArray | TyMap
//     …and the same for TyNullable, TyUnion, TyLit, TyNeg.
//
// That is the reject-parity work README.vl warns a false-reject closure costs, and it does
// not fit in this change. The two ways out were BOTH measured and BOTH cost something:
//
//   1. DELETE THE DEAD ARMS. Then the source no longer compiles under the OLD seed —
//      `non-exhaustive match — missing TyUnion, TyNullable, TyVar, TyLit, TyNeg` — so the
//      change cannot bootstrap. A `_` wildcard compiles under both and destroys the
//      property that match is deliberately `_`-less for: a 12th arena variant currently
//      breaks the self-compile HERE instead of silently answering "not assignable".
//   2. TOLERATE THE DEAD ARM — accept a pattern that names a member of the scrutinee's
//      DECLARED type which narrowing removed, and keep exhaustiveness on the narrowed set.
//      This is the right shape and it bootstraps (the source compiles unchanged under both
//      checkers), but it is a language-design slice in **A-exhaust** territory ("dead-arm
//      flagging", ROADMAP Track A) with its own unanswered question: `desugarMatchAt`
//      lowers the match to an if-chain, so a tolerated dead arm still emits an `is` test
//      against a member the emitter's narrowed set no longer holds.
//
// EXPECTED: compiles + runs, `f(true)` returns `true`.
// ACTUAL: `return type mismatch: expected boolean, got string | boolean`.
//
// TODO(completeness): land dead-arm tolerance (route 2) FIRST, then delete the `pgKeys`
// bank. When that happens, flip to `// @run` + `// @log true`, drop @error and the
// prefix, and add back the four residual shapes listed above as controls — they are known
// to work, so they cost nothing to pin.
//
// NOTE on the old `@error Codegen error` text this file carried: it was the retired TS
// host's wording, which is why the case sat in `EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES` in
// tests/cases_wasm_test.ts. The native checker rejects earlier, at the type tier, and the
// @error above now pins THAT — the divergence entry names the same fact.
function f(x: string | i32 | boolean): boolean {
  if x is i32 { return false }
  if x is string { return false }
  return x
}
print(f(true))
